function censusEvidence(sectionId) {
  if (sectionId === 'national') {
    return `<section class="argument-strip" aria-labelledby="national-reading">
      <h2 class="argument-caption" id="national-reading">Reading the census</h2>
      <ul class="argument-points">
        <li><strong>How many workers?</strong>
          Factories in the thirteen-state South employed about 209,000 wage earners in 1880 and 1.34 million in 1929. The region gained ground unevenly. Its share of US factory employment reached 14 percent in 1909, then slipped to 13 percent in 1919.
          <button type="button" class="argument-source" data-source="bls1947">Poulton 1947, 6–7, tables 1–2</button>
        </li>
        <li><strong>Why 0.77?</strong>
          Divide the South’s 11.7% share of US product value by its 15.2% share of wage earners. The same calculation gives 1.01 for 1860 and 0.66 for 1899. Material prices and the mix of goods affect the result, even if output per hour stays constant.
          <button type="button" class="argument-source" data-source="bls1947">Poulton 1947, 7, table 2</button>
        </li>
        <li><strong>Where did the work count?</strong>
          Draper built looms in Massachusetts for mills in South Carolina. Building the loom and weaving on it counted as factory work in different states.
          <button type="button" class="argument-source" data-source="draper1905">Draper 1905, title page and PDF 229, 233, 236</button>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>`;
  }

  if (sectionId === 'conclusion') {
    return `<section class="argument-strip" aria-labelledby="conclusion-reading">
      <h2 class="argument-caption" id="conclusion-reading">Equipping a mill after emancipation</h2>
      <p>Paying a machinery supplier partly in shares reduced the cash a Southern mill needed up front. Local investment could expand weaving in the South while orders for looms kept New England machine builders at work.</p>
      <p>Census takers counted spindles where mills used them to spin yarn. Those figures locate spinning capacity. To locate design work, Sutthiphisal followed the people named on textile patents and identified machinery firms that employed them.</p>
      <div class="citations">
        <button type="button" class="argument-source" data-source="bls1947">Poulton 1947, 9, table 3</button>
        <button type="button" class="argument-source" data-source="mitchell1918">Mitchell 1918, 205–15</button>
        <button type="button" class="argument-source" data-source="sutthiphisal2006">Sutthiphisal 2006, PDF 19–21</button>
        <a href="#sources/course">Muñoz, lecture IV, slide 4</a>
      </div>
    </section>`;
  }

  return '';
}

